{0 Requests and responses}

This page covers the types of one HTTP exchange: the {!Workero.Request}, the
{!Workero.Response}, the {!Workero.URL} and the {!Workero.Method}. It also
covers JSON and the outgoing {!Workero.fetch}.

{@ocaml[
let handler req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let url = Request.url req in
  match (Request.method_ req, URL.path url) with
  | GET, "/hello" ->
      let name = Option.value (URL.query url "name") ~default:"world" in
      Response.text (Printf.sprintf "hello, %s\n" name)
  | GET, "/agent" ->
      let agent = Option.value (Request.header req "user-agent") ~default:"?" in
      Response.text ~headers:[ ("x-agent", agent) ] "ok\n"
  | GET, "/where" ->
      let country = Option.value (Cf.country (Request.cf req)) ~default:"??" in
      Response.text (country ^ "\n")
  | POST, "/upload" -> Response.make ~status:201 (Request.bytes req)
  | GET, "/page" -> Response.html "<h1>hello</h1>"
  | GET, "/old" -> Response.redirect ~status:301 "https://example.com/new"
  | GET, "/done" -> Response.text ~status:204 ""
  | _ -> Response.text ~status:404 "not found\n"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

{1 Method}

{!Workero.Method.t} is a variant: [GET], [HEAD], [POST], [PUT], [DELETE],
[PATCH], [OPTIONS] or [Other of string]. Match on {!Workero.Request.method_}.
{!Workero.Method.of_string} is case-sensitive, as HTTP is. A name that is not
one of the seven becomes [Other name].

{1 Request}

- {!Workero.Request.url} is the {!Workero.URL.t}.
- {!Workero.Request.header} reads one header. The name is not case-sensitive.
  Repeated headers are joined with [", "].
- {!Workero.Request.headers} lists all headers with lower case names, sorted by
  name.
- {!Workero.Request.text} reads the body as UTF-8 text. Invalid UTF-8 is
  replaced.
- {!Workero.Request.bytes} reads the body as raw bytes, in a [string].
- {!Workero.Request.cf} gives the {!Workero.Cf.t}, the Cloudflare properties of
  the request.

{!Workero.Cf} has [country], [region], [city], [colo], [timezone] and [asn].
Each one is an option. It is [None] when the runtime gives no value, for
example in a local test.

{1 Response}

- {!Workero.Response.text} sends a UTF-8 text body. [status] is 200 by default.
- {!Workero.Response.html} is the same with [content-type: text/html;
  charset=utf-8].
- {!Workero.Response.make} sends the bytes as they are, with no [content-type].
- {!Workero.Response.redirect} sends a [location] header. The URL must be
  absolute, and the status 301, 302, 303, 307 or 308 (302 by default). It
  raises [Invalid_argument] for another status.

Pass [~headers] as a list of pairs. A [content-type] in [headers] replaces the
default one.

A status that allows no body (101, 204, 205 and 304) works only with an empty
body. The runtime rejects a response with one of these statuses and a body.

A response that you got from {!Workero.fetch} has readers:
{!Workero.Response.status}, {!Workero.Response.header},
{!Workero.Response.headers}, {!Workero.Response.body_text} and
{!Workero.Response.body_bytes}.

{1 URL}

{!Workero.URL} wraps [Js_of_ocaml.Url]. It parses [http] and [https] URLs.
{!Workero.URL.path} is decoded and starts with ["/"].
{!Workero.URL.query}, {!Workero.URL.query_all} and {!Workero.URL.query_args}
give decoded values, and ["+"] in a query is a space.

It differs from the WHATWG [URL] of JavaScript:

- A query key without [=], such as [?a], is dropped.
- The path is decoded, so [%2F] and ["/"] look the same.
- {!Workero.URL.to_string} encodes again: [%zz] becomes [%25zz], and a space in
  the query becomes [%20].
- There is no [search], [hash] or [origin].
- {!Workero.URL.of_string} gives [None] for any scheme other than [http] and
  [https].
- {!Workero.Request.url} raises [Invalid_argument] when the URL has a host with
  ["_"], or userinfo as in [http://user:pw@host/].

{1 JSON}

{!Workero.JSON} reads and writes JSON with
{{:https://github.com/melange-community/jsonkit}jsonkit}. A JSON value is a
[Jsonkit.t]. {!Workero.JSON} does not re-export jsonkit. opam installs jsonkit
with workero, but you add [jsonkit] to the [libraries] of your [dune] file to
build or decode values.

{@ocaml[
open Jsonkit.Primitives

type user = { name : string; age : int } [@@deriving json]

let handler req _env _ctx =
  match Workero.JSON.decode user_of_json req with
  | Ok user -> Workero.JSON.response (user_to_json user)
  | Error message -> Workero.JSON.error message

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Add [jsonkit] to [libraries], and [jsonkit.ppx] to [preprocess] for
[[@@deriving json]]:

{@dune[
(executable
 (name main)
 (modes wasm)
 (preprocess
  (pps jsonkit.ppx))
 (libraries workero jsonkit))
]}

- {!Workero.JSON.decode} reads the body and applies a decoder. It gives
  [Error message] for invalid JSON or when the decoder raises.
- {!Workero.JSON.parse} reads the body and gives the [Jsonkit.t].
- {!Workero.JSON.request} is [parse] that raises [Jsonkit.Of_string_error]. An
  escaping exception makes the response a 500.
- {!Workero.JSON.response} sends JSON with status 200 and
  [content-type: application/json; charset=utf-8].
- {!Workero.JSON.error} sends [{"error": message}] with status 400.

Without the ppx, write the decoder with the combinators of
[Jsonkit.Of_json]. See {!Workero.JSON} for an example.

{1 Calling other servers}

{!Workero.fetch} sends an HTTP request and waits for the response headers.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  let res =
    fetch ~method_:POST
      ~headers:[ ("content-type", "application/json") ]
      ~body:{|{"ping":true}|} "https://example.com/api"
  in
  match Response.status res with
  | 200 -> Response.text (Response.body_text res)
  | status -> Response.text ~status:502 (Printf.sprintf "upstream %d\n" status)

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

- [method_] is [GET] by default.
- [body] is a [string] of raw bytes. It sets no [content-type]: add one in
  [headers].
- [Invalid_argument] is raised when [body] is given with [GET] or [HEAD].
- {!Workero.Js_error} is raised when the request fails, for example on a
  network error.
- Read the body with {!Workero.Response.body_text} or
  {!Workero.Response.body_bytes}.

Call {!Workero.fetch} only from a handler or from a function given to
{!Workero.Context.wait_until}.
